  "notes": [
    "The source is one continuous cycle, its own title page reading \"Advent 2025 through Christ the King 2026\" (Nov. 30, 2025 - Nov. 22, 2026). Per this schema's own \"one file per diocese per year\" rule and this corpus's existing precedent for the identical shape of source (episcopal-atlanta-2026.json and episcopal-north-carolina-2026.json, both of which deferred a similarly small late-2025 Advent lead-in rather than splitting into a second file), only the 48 weeks landing in calendar 2026 (January 1 - November 22) are included here. The six 2025 weeks are deliberately excluded, not fabricated or force-fit: Nov. 30, 2025 (First Sunday of Advent) - St. Stephen's, Guymon / The Anglican Church of Australia; Dec. 7 (Second Sunday of Advent) - St. John's, Woodward; Grace, Yukon / The Church of Bangladesh; Dec. 14 (Third Sunday of Advent) - St. Matthew's, Enid / Igreja Episcopal Anglicana do Brasil; Dec. 21 (Fourth Sunday of Advent) - St. Paul's, Clinton; Whirlwind Mission, Watonga / The Anglican Church of Burundi; Dec. 25 (Christmas Day) - The Diocese of Sapele – The Anglican Church of Nigeria; Dec. 28 (First Sunday after Christmas) - St. James', Oklahoma City / The Anglican Church of Canada. They would need their own episcopal-oklahoma-2025.json if ever ingested.",
    "Source is a two-column-per-page PDF (5 pages). Reading order runs top-to-bottom down the left column, then top-to-bottom down the right column, before continuing onto the next page -- confirmed by rendering all 5 pages as images (150 dpi) and visually checking the column order and date-to-subject pairing against pdftotext -layout's linearized extraction before trusting it, per this corpus's standing rule for multi-column sources.",
    "Every week pairs one or more local subjects (a parish, mission, school, ministry, or diocesan body) with a final line naming a partner church/diocese elsewhere in the worldwide Anglican Communion (e.g. 'The Anglican Church of Kenya', or 'The Diocese of Sapele – The Anglican Church of Nigeria'). Since this file's scope is 'diocese' (the Diocese of Oklahoma's own cycle of its parishes), not 'communion', these worldwide-partner lines use subject type 'category' with the source's full line kept verbatim as one `name` (not split into this schema's separate 'diocese'/'province' fields, which apply only to scope-'communion' files).",
    "Three campus ministries in the source ('St. Augustine Canterbury - OSU Campus Ministry', Jan. 1; 'St. Anselm Canterbury - OU Campus Ministry', Mar. 8) name no town/city, only a university abbreviation -- recorded as `category` rather than `parish` since this schema's 'parish' type requires an explicit place and none is given.",
    "Three schools named with an explicit city ('Holland Hall School, Tulsa'; 'Casady School, Oklahoma City'; 'Oak Hall School, Ardmore', all Sept. 27) and 'St. Mary's School, Edmond' (Jan. 18) are recorded as `category` subjects with the city folded into `name`, not `parish`, since a school is not itself a congregation -- consistent with this corpus's existing precedent (episcopal-delaware-2027.json's 'St. Anne's School, Middletown').",
    "'Ardmore Village, Ardmore' (May 31, alongside St. Philip's, Ardmore) and 'St. Simeon's Home, Tulsa' (Aug. 30, alongside St. John's, Tulsa) read as senior-living/residential communities rather than congregations, and 'St. Crispin's Conference Center, Wewoka' (July 19) as a diocesan conference center rather than a congregation -- all three recorded as `category` with the place folded into `name`, not `parish`.",
    "liturgicalNote is transcribed in ordinary title case (e.g. 'Fourth Sunday of Advent'), not the source's own all-caps underlined heading style, which reads as a font/heading-style effect of the Word-to-PDF export rather than the source's own literal letter-casing -- consistent with how this corpus already renders liturgicalNote elsewhere in title case rather than shouting caps.",
    "Nov. 22's own header prints 'NOVEMBER 22NDLAST SUNDAY AFTER PENTOCOST: CHRIST TH E KING'. The run-together '22ND'+'LAST' with no space is the same ordinal-superscript kerning artifact seen throughout the rest of the source (e.g. '1ST', '4TH' always render tight against the following word) and is not treated as content. 'PENTOCOST', however, is a genuine misspelling of 'Pentecost' in the source's own wording -- preserved verbatim in this entry's liturgicalNote as 'Last Sunday after Pentocost: Christ the King', not silently corrected.",
    "Aug. 23 and Aug. 30 print 'THIRTEENTH DAY AFTER PENTECOST (Proper 16)' and 'FOURTEENTH DAY AFTER PENTECOST (Proper 17)' -- 'Day' rather than the 'Sunday' used in every other week's heading (both dates are in fact Sundays) -- preserved as printed, not corrected to 'Sunday'.",
    "Nov. 1's header reads 'ALL SAINTS or TWENTY-THIRD SUNDAY AFTER PENTECOST (Proper 26)', offering both names for the day as the source itself does; both are kept in this entry's liturgicalNote exactly as given.",
    "May 24: the source prints the town as \"Paul's Valley\" (with an apostrophe); the actual Oklahoma town is spelled \"Pauls Valley\" (no apostrophe). Preserved as printed per this corpus's standing rule against silently correcting a source's own text, disclosed here as a likely source typo rather than corrected.",
    "Diacritics are preserved exactly as printed in the source, including where they omit a mark a fuller spelling would carry (e.g. 'Igreja Anglicana de Mocambique e Angola' for Moçambique, 'La Iglesia Anglicana de Mexico' for México, 'Eglise Anglicane du Rwanda' for Église, 'Iglesia Anglicana de la Region Central de America' for Región) -- not silently accented to a 'more correct' spelling.",
    "The diocese's own full name, 'The Episcopal Diocese of Oklahoma', is taken verbatim from the source's own title page."
